// Compiled by bin/build-help.php from help/*.md (the add-on's topics only). Do not edit.
export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"modelcraft","addon":"factory","name":"Factory floor","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"factory-start","scope":"addon:factory","title":"Factory floor: getting started","summary":"Turn the Material flow layout into a list of machines and cells, read them live or from the simulator, and see them over the layout.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["factory","shop floor","machines","cells","monitoring","scada","operator screen","mtconnect","oee"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"switch-it-on","text":"Switch it on","level":2},{"id":"a-first-run-with-the-simulator","text":"A first run with the simulator","level":2},{"id":"cells","text":"Cells","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFactory floor\u003C/span\u003E is an add-on for ModelCraft that sits beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E. Each station in the flow layout becomes a machine. You group machines into cells, choose how each one is read, watch them live, keep a queue of programs for them, and get a history with simple OEE.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt reads first.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing in this add-on writes to a machine or a PLC unless you switch writing on for that one device and say yes to each write. This build has no way to write at all, so a write is refused and says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot a safety system.\u003C/strong\u003E It is never part of a safety chain. The machines&#039; own guards, interlocks and emergency stops are the only safety functions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"switch-it-on\"\u003ESwitch it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, and switch on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFactory floor\u003C/span\u003E (and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E if you want the machines to come from a layout). The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFactory floor\u003C/span\u003E section appears in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-first-run-with-the-simulator\"\u003EA first run with the simulator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExample line\u003C/span\u003E. Its stations show up under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMachines and cells\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESimulate every machine\u003C/span\u003E. Each machine is now a seeded simulated machine: it makes parts, waits now and then, breaks down at random and is repaired.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart reading\u003C/span\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E table shows each machine&#039;s state, its parts (bad parts in brackets), its OEE and a trend of its spindle speed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the operator screen over the layout\u003C/span\u003E. A label floats over each station in the 3D view with its state, parts, OEE, values and any alarm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESimulator speed\u003C/span\u003E to 60× to see an hour in a minute. The speed is offered only while every machine is simulated.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same seed gives the same history, so a run can be repeated.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cells\"\u003ECells\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a cell\u003C/span\u003E makes a new cell; rename it in its box. Pick a machine (its button under the cell) to open its settings, and choose its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECell\u003C/span\u003E there.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Factory floor is an add-on for ModelCraft that sits beside Material flow. Each station in the flow layout becomes a machine. You group machines into cells, choose how each one is read, watch them live, keep a queue of programs for them, and get a history with simple OEE. It reads first. Nothing in this add-on writes to a machine or a PLC unless you switch writing on for that one device and say yes to each write. This build has no way to write at all, so a write is refused and says so. Not a safety system. It is never part of a safety chain. The machines' own guards, interlocks and emergency stops are the only safety functions. Switch it on Open Settings, then Add-ons, and switch on Factory floor (and Material flow if you want the machines to come from a layout). The Factory floor section appears in the right panel. A first run with the simulator In Material flow, choose Example line. Its stations show up under Machines and cells. Choose Simulate every machine. Each machine is now a seeded simulated machine: it makes parts, waits now and then, breaks down at random and is repaired. Choose Start reading. The Live table shows each machine's state, its parts (bad parts in brackets), its OEE and a trend of its spindle speed. Tick Show the operator screen over the layout. A label floats over each station in the 3D view with its state, parts, OEE, values and any alarm. Set Simulator speed to 60× to see an hour in a minute. The speed is offered only while every machine is simulated. The same seed gives the same history, so a run can be repeated. Cells Add a cell makes a new cell; rename it in its box. Pick a machine (its button under the cell) to open its settings, and choose its Cell there."},{"slug":"factory-connect","scope":"addon:factory","title":"Factory floor: reading real machines","summary":"Read a machine through MTConnect, or through OPC UA, MQTT, Modbus or BACnet in CadCraft Studio, and what the states mean.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["mtconnect","agent","probe","current","sample","opc ua","mqtt","modbus","bacnet","machine state","part count"],"headings":[{"id":"mtconnect","text":"MTConnect","level":2},{"id":"opc-ua-mqtt-modbus-tcp-bacnet-ip","text":"OPC UA, MQTT, Modbus TCP, BACnet/IP","level":2},{"id":"states","text":"States","level":2},{"id":"values-limits-and-alarms","text":"Values, limits and alarms","level":2},{"id":"writing","text":"Writing","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"mtconnect\"\u003EMTConnect\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick a machine and set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead by\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMTConnect\u003C/span\u003E. Type the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgent address\u003C/span\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003Ehttp://192.168.1.20:5000\u003C/code\u003E) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EList the agent&#039;s devices\u003C/span\u003E, then pick the device.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe add-on reads the agent&#039;s public documents: the device list (\u003Ccode\u003Eprobe\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ccode\u003Ecurrent\u003C/code\u003E once, then \u003Ccode\u003Esample\u003C/code\u003E from the next sequence number on every read, so no change between two reads is missed. Each change is recorded at the time the agent gives it. If the agent restarts or its buffer no longer holds what came in between, it reads \u003Ccode\u003Ecurrent\u003C/code\u003E again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere it can read from.\u003C/strong\u003E A web page can only read an agent over https that allows this site. Most shop-floor agents are plain http on your own network: read them in CadCraft Studio. An address that is not on a private network needs \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EI approve this host\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opc-ua-mqtt-modbus-tcp-bacnet-ip\"\u003EOPC UA, MQTT, Modbus TCP, BACnet/IP\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese need CadCraft Studio: a web page cannot open a plant network connection. Type the host and port, then the tags: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EState tag\u003C/span\u003E (a word such as \u003Ccode\u003EACTIVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREADY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESTOPPED\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EFAULT\u003C/code\u003E, or a number: 0 not seen, 1 idle, 2 running, 3 stopped, 4 down, 5 setup), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart count tag\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGood parts tag\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBad parts tag\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlarm tag\u003C/span\u003E. Without Studio the machine says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENeeds CadCraft Studio.\u003C/span\u003E and nothing is sent.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"states\"\u003EStates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EState\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhen\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERunning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe program is running (MTConnect execution ACTIVE)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIdle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eready, waiting, or the program finished\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStopped\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Estopped, feed hold, interrupted, optional stop\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESetup\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ein manual, manual data input or edit mode and not running\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDown\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea fault condition or an emergency stop\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot seen\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe machine or its agent did not answer, or says it is unavailable\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlanned stop\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyou marked it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlanned stop\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"values-limits-and-alarms\"\u003EValues, limits and alarms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumeric values (spindle speed, load and the like) are kept with a trend. Open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELimits on n values\u003C/span\u003E in a machine&#039;s settings to give a value a low or high limit: going past it shows an alarm in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E table and on the operator screen. A machine&#039;s own fault and warning conditions are alarms too, with their text and code.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"writing\"\u003EWriting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow writing to this device\u003C/span\u003E is off for every device. Switching it on asks first, and every write asks again. This build has no channel that writes to a machine or PLC, so even then the write is refused and says so.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"MTConnect Pick a machine and set Read by to MTConnect. Type the Agent address (for example http://192.168.1.20:5000) and choose List the agent's devices, then pick the device. The add-on reads the agent's public documents: the device list (probe), then current once, then sample from the next sequence number on every read, so no change between two reads is missed. Each change is recorded at the time the agent gives it. If the agent restarts or its buffer no longer holds what came in between, it reads current again. Where it can read from. A web page can only read an agent over https that allows this site. Most shop-floor agents are plain http on your own network: read them in CadCraft Studio. An address that is not on a private network needs I approve this host. OPC UA, MQTT, Modbus TCP, BACnet/IP These need CadCraft Studio: a web page cannot open a plant network connection. Type the host and port, then the tags: State tag (a word such as ACTIVE, READY, STOPPED, FAULT, or a number: 0 not seen, 1 idle, 2 running, 3 stopped, 4 down, 5 setup), Part count tag, Good parts tag, Bad parts tag and Alarm tag. Without Studio the machine says Needs CadCraft Studio. and nothing is sent. States State When Running the program is running (MTConnect execution ACTIVE) Idle ready, waiting, or the program finished Stopped stopped, feed hold, interrupted, optional stop Setup in manual, manual data input or edit mode and not running Down a fault condition or an emergency stop Not seen the machine or its agent did not answer, or says it is unavailable Planned stop you marked it with Planned stop Values, limits and alarms Numeric values (spindle speed, load and the like) are kept with a trend. Open Limits on n values in a machine's settings to give a value a low or high limit: going past it shows an alarm in the Live table and on the operator screen. A machine's own fault and warning conditions are alarms too, with their text and code. Writing Allow writing to this device is off for every device. Switching it on asks first, and every write asks again. This build has no channel that writes to a machine or PLC, so even then the write is refused and says so."},{"slug":"factory-jobs","scope":"addon:factory","title":"Factory floor: the job queue","summary":"Queue posted programs per machine, hand them over from PartCraft, check them and send them with CadCraft Studio's Send to a machine.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["job queue","g-code","nc program","post","cam","send to machine","partcraft","dnc"],"headings":[{"id":"put-a-job-in-the-queue","text":"Put a job in the queue","level":2},{"id":"check-and-send","text":"Check and send","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"put-a-job-in-the-queue\"\u003EPut a job in the queue\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a program file…\u003C/span\u003E adds a G-code or NC file (\u003Ccode\u003E.nc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.ngc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.gcode\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tap\u003C/code\u003E and the like) as a job.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFrom PartCraft: switch on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFactory floor\u003C/span\u003E add-on in PartCraft, post a program in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EManufacture\u003C/span\u003E, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to the factory job queue\u003C/span\u003E. Back in ModelCraft, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake jobs from PartCraft\u003C/span\u003E brings it in. This hand-over works between apps open in the same browser. Elsewhere, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as a job file\u003C/span\u003E in PartCraft and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a job file…\u003C/span\u003E here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-and-send\"\u003ECheck and send\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick the job&#039;s machine. The queue warns when a job was posted for another control than the machine&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EControl (post)\u003C/span\u003E, when the machine is down or not seen, and refuses a file the machine does not take.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend…\u003C/span\u003E opens CadCraft Studio&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend to a machine\u003C/span\u003E dialog, where you pick the machine set up in Studio&#039;s Machines settings. The file is uploaded to the machine. \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing here starts a machine:\u003C/strong\u003E start it at the machine. In a web browser there is no Send; you can still queue, check and save jobs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMark done\u003C/span\u003E when the part is made. A failed send can be queued again.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Put a job in the queue Open a program file… adds a G-code or NC file (.nc, .ngc, .gcode, .tap and the like) as a job. From PartCraft: switch on the Factory floor add-on in PartCraft, post a program in Manufacture, then choose Add to the factory job queue. Back in ModelCraft, Take jobs from PartCraft brings it in. This hand-over works between apps open in the same browser. Elsewhere, use Save as a job file in PartCraft and Open a job file… here. Check and send Pick the job's machine. The queue warns when a job was posted for another control than the machine's Control (post), when the machine is down or not seen, and refuses a file the machine does not take. Send… opens CadCraft Studio's Send to a machine dialog, where you pick the machine set up in Studio's Machines settings. The file is uploaded to the machine. Nothing here starts a machine: start it at the machine. In a web browser there is no Send; you can still queue, check and save jobs. Mark done when the part is made. A failed send can be queued again."},{"slug":"factory-oee","scope":"addon:factory","title":"Factory floor: history and OEE","summary":"How the add-on records states, parts and alarms, how simple OEE is worked out, and what is not supported.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["oee","availability","performance","quality","history","historian","csv","downtime"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-recorded","text":"What is recorded","level":2},{"id":"oee","text":"OEE","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-recorded\"\u003EWhat is recorded\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile reading, each machine&#039;s state is kept as time spans, with its part counts and its alarms as they come and go. The history stays in this browser; the machine settings and the job queue are saved in the model file with the layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"oee\"\u003EOEE\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAvailability\u003C/strong\u003E = running time ÷ planned time. Planned time is the period less planned stops and less time the machine was not seen (nobody knows what it did then).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPerformance\u003C/strong\u003E = ideal cycle time × parts made ÷ running time. The ideal cycle time comes from the flow station&#039;s mean process time, or what you type. Above 100 % means the ideal time is too long; the OEE then uses 100 %.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQuality\u003C/strong\u003E = good parts ÷ parts made.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOEE\u003C/strong\u003E = availability × performance × quality.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA machine&#039;s counters are read as running totals. A counter that goes down was reset at the machine, and counting starts again from zero.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave OEE (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave state history (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E write files; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear history\u003C/span\u003E starts again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot a safety system and never part of a safety chain.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo writing to machines or PLCs in this build. No starting of programs from here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReal machines were not tried: MTConnect was tested against our own simulator, served over HTTP on the test computer; OPC UA, MQTT, Modbus and BACnet need CadCraft Studio&#039;s device bridge, which is not built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo connection to a plant historian, no shift calendars in OEE (use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlanned stop\u003C/span\u003E), no reports by shift or by product, no MTConnect assets (tools, programs).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What is recorded While reading, each machine's state is kept as time spans, with its part counts and its alarms as they come and go. The history stays in this browser; the machine settings and the job queue are saved in the model file with the layout. OEE Availability = running time ÷ planned time. Planned time is the period less planned stops and less time the machine was not seen (nobody knows what it did then). Performance = ideal cycle time × parts made ÷ running time. The ideal cycle time comes from the flow station's mean process time, or what you type. Above 100 % means the ideal time is too long; the OEE then uses 100 %. Quality = good parts ÷ parts made. OEE = availability × performance × quality. A machine's counters are read as running totals. A counter that goes down was reset at the machine, and counting starts again from zero. Save OEE (CSV) and Save state history (CSV) write files; Clear history starts again. Limits Not a safety system and never part of a safety chain. No writing to machines or PLCs in this build. No starting of programs from here. Real machines were not tried: MTConnect was tested against our own simulator, served over HTTP on the test computer; OPC UA, MQTT, Modbus and BACnet need CadCraft Studio's device bridge, which is not built yet. No connection to a plant historian, no shift calendars in OEE (use Planned stop), no reports by shift or by product, no MTConnect assets (tools, programs)."}]};
